  returns an error (lines 234-238) -- real error injection, not a stub name.

LOCK ORDERING -- sound, single order s.mu -> cacheMu.
  $ sed -n on migration.go: EnableEncryptionWithProvider (line 25) and
    DisableEncryption (line 116) both open with `s.mu.Lock(); defer
    s.mu.Unlock()` -- held for the whole run.
  $ invalidateCache (storage.go:243-248) takes only s.cacheMu.Lock/Unlock.
  $ script-extracted all five cacheMu critical sections in the package
    (storage.go:226, 244, 253, 263, 303) and grepped each for `s.mu`:
      every one reported "s.mu inside = NONE".
    The near miss is ReadFileContext: cacheMu.RLock at 303, RUnlock at 308 and
    311, s.mu.RLock only at 314 -- after the release, never nested.
  $ grep of all 11 s.mu acquisitions shows the only nesting is the other way
    (Lock() at storage.go:214 -> cacheMu.Lock at 226; migration.go:25/116 ->
    invalidateCache). No path takes cacheMu and then wants s.mu, so ABBA is
    structurally impossible.
  Runtime corroboration: scratch test, 8 goroutines hammering ReadFile against
  repeated EnableEncryption/DisableEncryption cycles, -race, 180s timeout:
    --- PASS: TestCheckerLockOrderNoDeadlock (42.72s)
    "completed 1 enable/disable cycles against 8 concurrent readers with no
    deadlock", contents intact afterwards, race detector silent.

FINDING (does not fail a numbered point, recorded per the brief's standing
instruction about undefended lines).
  Single-call mutation: I removed each of the six invalidateCache calls one at
  a time and ran the full package suite each time.
    migration.go:253 (encrypt, pre-transform)  -> FAIL
      TestEncryptFailureDoesNotLeavePlaintextCached          DEFENDED
    migration.go:274 (encrypt, post-rename)    -> ok, 39.693s  UNDEFENDED
    migration.go:295 (decrypt, pre-transform)  -> ok, 39.674s  UNDEFENDED
    migration.go:313 (decrypt, post-rename)    -> ok, 39.770s  UNDEFENDED
    migration.go:335 (rollback, pre-transform) -> ok, 39.853s  UNDEFENDED
    migration.go:346 (rollback, post-rename)   -> ok, 39.913s  UNDEFENDED
  Five of six survive single removal. The reason is masking, not absence of
  coverage: each helper brackets the transform on both sides, so with either
  member of a pair present the property still holds on the success path, and
  publishCache's generation check independently refuses a late publish. Removing
  a whole pair does fail a named test in every case (see points 1-3), and
  removing all six fails four tests at once:
    $ mutant all_six -> FAIL Enable, Disable, Rollback, EncryptFailure.
  The one call that is individually load-bearing is exactly the one the lead
  added a test for. The other five are documented defence-in-depth ("Mutations
  invalidate on BOTH sides", invalidateCache doc) that no single test pins.
  This is the same shape as the previously recorded correct-but-untested
  CreateExclusive invalidation; naming it, not treating it as a criterion miss,
  since points 1-3 state properties and each property is mutation-confirmed.

